# Host-global analysis-platform configuration.
#
# Sourced, never executed. One Dom0 has one analysis bridge, one uplink,
# one VLAN address space and one volume group, so these values have
# exactly one correct setting per host -- not one per target OS.
#
# Every consumer resolves its own location first and sources this file
# alongside its per-OS one:
#
#     SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/platform.env"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/pipeline-ubuntu.env"
#
# Values are written as ${NAME:-default}, so anything already set in the
# environment wins and CI can override without patching this file.
#
# --- Why this file exists (issue #6, decision 2) ------------------------
#
# Three copies of pipeline-<os>.env would mean three places that must
# agree about the bridge name, with nothing checking that they do. Two
# pipelines disagreeing about ANALYSIS_BRIDGE is not a misconfiguration
# that fails loudly -- it is a clone attached to a bridge with no
# isolation rules on it, which looks like a working analysis.
#
# C4 in docs/architecture/analysis-pipeline.md therefore forbids
# pipeline-<os>.env from defining any name below, and the check is a
# grep:
#
#     grep -nE '^(VG_NAME|ANALYSIS_BRIDGE|UPLINK_BRIDGE|SUBNET_PREFIX)' \
#         automation/pipeline-*.env      # expect: no output
#
# The test for whether a value belongs here is not "how many scripts read
# it" but "would two OS pipelines ever be right to disagree". CLONE_LV_SIZE
# used to be the worked example of a value that passed it -- Android's 32G
# against Ubuntu's 64G being a property of the guest image -- until thin
# snapshots retired it entirely on 2026-08-29. MAX_CLONES deliberately stays in
# pipeline-<os>.env despite being host-global, because DRAFT-041 makes it
# host-global outright -- one sandboxd, one pool, no per-OS referent -- and
# moving it twice is worse than moving it once.
#
# --- GENERATED VALUES: edit sandboxd/config.py, not this file -----------
#
# Every assignment below is written by `scripts/gen-xen-configs.py` from
# `sandboxd/config.py`, which is the source of truth and where an operator
# tunes this host. **A hand-edit here is reverted on the next run**, and
# `sandboxd/tests/test_xen_configs.py` fails until the two agree.
#
#     scripts/gen-xen-configs.py            # rewrite from config.py
#     scripts/gen-xen-configs.py --check    # exit 1 if this file drifted
#
# The comments are NOT generated -- only the values on the assignment
# lines are rewritten, so everything explaining a value survives.
#
# Why the source is Python and not this file: shell cannot be imported by
# `sandboxd`, and Xen `.cfg` files can read neither. One of the three has
# to win, and the one an operator already opens to change a timeout is
# the least surprising place to put the rest.
#
# `${NAME:-default}` is preserved on purpose: an exported variable still
# wins at runtime, so CI can override without patching anything.

# --- Networking ---------------------------------------------------------

# The analysis bridge. VLAN sub-interfaces <bridge>.<vlan> are created on
# it, one per clone slot, and every isolation rule matches on the
# `<bridge>.+` wildcard rather than on anything guest-specific.
#
# This script does not create it -- `ovs-vsctl add-br xenbr1` is a
# one-time install step, and network-setup.sh fails with a message naming
# that step if the bridge is absent.
ANALYSIS_BRIDGE="${ANALYSIS_BRIDGE:-xenbr1}"

# The uplink to the Internet. Clone egress is narrowed to this interface:
# traffic out of a VLAN sub-interface to anywhere other than the uplink is
# dropped, which is what stops clone-to-clone and clone-to-LAN traffic.
UPLINK_BRIDGE="${UPLINK_BRIDGE:-xenbr0}"

# First two octets of the per-VLAN gateway scheme, <prefix>.<vlan>.1/24.
# The third octet IS the VLAN id, which is why network-setup.sh rejects a
# clone count above 255.
#
# This is NOT the RFC1918 range used in the isolation drop list. That list
# covers 172.16.0.0/12 among others and is about what a clone may reach,
# not about where its own gateway lives; the two are independent and the
# drop list stays hard-coded.
SUBNET_PREFIX="${SUBNET_PREFIX:-172.16}"

# --- CPU partition ------------------------------------------------------
#
# **Xen sees 24 online pCPUs, not 32.** `smt=0` parks all eight P-core
# siblings, leaving 8 P-cores as the EVENS 0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14 and 16
# E-cores as 16-31. Everything below is in terms of that online set, and
# that is the trap: a range resolves against ONLINE pCPUs, so the
# nested-era `cpus="6-11"` silently meant {6,8,10} -- three cores, all
# three of them Dom0's. Enumerate P-cores explicitly; ranges are safe only
# across 16-31, where no sibling is parked.
#
# The split is the hardware boundary, adopted 2026-08-31: Dom0 and DRAKVUF
# on the fast cores, guests on the slow ones. Defensible because
# 12-baremetal-trial.md F7 measured clone vCPUs paused ~93% of wall time,
# and H2 measured clones on E-cores at -0.5% against the P-core baseline
# -- inside the 0.6% run-to-run spread, so no measurable cost. C3a
# measured E-cores 1.7x slower on a busy loop, which is why the asymmetry
# matters for Dom0 and not for the guests.
#
# It also stops 14 of 24 cores sitting idle, which is what the previous
# partition did: Dom0 0-10, clones 12,14, platform 16,17, nothing on
# 18-31.

# Dom0's own pCPUs. **Not consumed by any generated file** -- Dom0's
# affinity comes from `dom0_max_vcpus` and `dom0_vcpus_pin` on the Xen
# boot line, which this repository does not own. Recorded here so the
# partition is readable in one place and a reviewer can check the boot
# line against it.
#
# `dom0_vcpus_pin` hands Dom0 the first N ONLINE pCPUs, so
# `dom0_max_vcpus=8` yields exactly the eight evens below. Verify with
# `xl vcpu-list 0` after any boot-line change: FINDING C-1 in
# 12-baremetal-trial.md records the sixth pin silently not taking on a
# sparse online set, leaving one vCPU floating on another vCPU's core.
DOM0_PCPUS="${DOM0_PCPUS:-0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14}"

# Analysis clones. Written into misc/*_sandbox.cfg by
# scripts/gen-xen-configs.py. All clones share this set; it is not divided
# per slot, and it does not need to be -- memory is the binding constraint
# on concurrency, not cores.
CLONE_PCPUS="${CLONE_PCPUS:-16-27}"

# The platform DomU. Disjoint from CLONE_PCPUS on purpose: it is a
# long-running service and clones are transient, so overlapping them would
# put analysis load on the thing recording the analysis.
PLATFORM_PCPUS="${PLATFORM_PCPUS:-28-31}"

# --- Storage ------------------------------------------------------------

# LVM volume group holding the golden images, the sandbox templates and
# every per-clone copy-on-write snapshot.
#
# Read by clone.pl from the environment rather than from this file
# directly -- it is Perl, invoked by dirwatch on a fixed 3-argument
# contract, so run_analysis-<os>.sh exports this for it.
VG_NAME="${VG_NAME:-vg-root}"